# Introduction

This task focuses on exploring model fitting, evaluation, and comparison using real-world datasets to deepen understanding of regression techniques and model validation. In Task 1, we analyze the Auto dataset from the ISLR package by fitting three models to explain the relationship between miles per gallon (mpg) and horsepower: a linear model, a quadratic polynomial, and a cubic polynomial. We compare the models using the validation set approach with two train/test splits (50/50 and 70/30), and evaluate their performance using metrics like Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), Mean Squared Error (MSE), and Median Absolute Deviation (MAD). Additionally, we employ Leave-One-Out Cross Validation (LOOCV), 5-fold, and 10-fold Cross Validation to systematically assess the models. In Task 2, we analyze the economics dataset from the ggplot2 package to model the relationship between the number of unemployed persons (unemploy) and the median duration of unemployment (uempmed). Using linear, exponential/logarithmic, and polynomial models (degrees 2, 3, and 10), we evaluate their fits and validate them through graphical comparisons and cross-validation methods. The task concludes with insights into concepts like underfitting, overfitting, and the role of different cross-validation approaches in selecting optimal models.

