### 1.1 Fit models

In this task, we aim to explore the relationship between miles per gallon (mpg) and horsepower in the Auto dataset from the ISLR package by fitting three different regression models: a linear model, a quadratic polynomial model, and a cubic polynomial model. To understand and compare the performance of these models, we will plot the data points (mpg vs. horsepower) and overlay the predictions from each model. This visualization will help us assess how well each model captures the underlying trends in the data and evaluate their suitability for explaining the relationship between the two variables.

``` {r}
linear_model <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ horsepower, data = Auto), error = function(e) NULL)
quadratic_model <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ poly(horsepower, 2), data = Auto), error = function(e) NULL)
cubic_model <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ poly(horsepower, 3), data = Auto), error = function(e) NULL)

if (!is.null(linear_model) & !is.null(quadratic_model) & !is.null(cubic_model)) {
  plot(Auto$horsepower, 
       Auto$mpg, 
       main = "mpg vs horsepower", 
       xlab = "Horsepower",
       ylab = "MPG", 
       pch = 16)

  lines(Auto$horsepower, predict(linear_model), col = "blue", lwd = 2)
  lines(Auto$horsepower, predict(quadratic_model), col = "red", lwd = 2)
  lines(Auto$horsepower, predict(cubic_model), col = "green", lwd = 2)

  legend("topright", 
         legend = c("Linear", "Quadratic", "Cubic"), 
         col = c("blue", "red", "green"), 
         lwd = 2)
} else {
  print("Error in fitting models")
}

```
The plot displays the fitting of the different models.

