### 1.2 Use the validation set approach to compare the models. Use once a train/test split of 50%/50% and once 70%/30%. Choose the best model based on Root Mean Squared Error, Mean Squared Error and Median Absolute Deviation.

In this task, we use the validation set approach to compare the performance of three regression models—linear, quadratic, and cubic—fitted to the relationship between mpg and horsepower in the Auto dataset. We split the data into training and test sets twice, with ratios of 50/50 and 70/30, and evaluate each model’s performance on the test set using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), Mean Squared Error (MSE), and Median Absolute Deviation (MAD). This comparison helps determine which model best balances accuracy and complexity for predicting mpg based on horsepower.

``` {r}
evaluate_model <- function(model, test_data) {
  tryCatch({
    predictions <- predict(model, newdata = test_data)
    actuals <- test_data$mpg
    mse <- mean((predictions - actuals)^2)
    rmse <- sqrt(mse)
    mad <- median(abs(predictions - actuals))
    
    return(c(MSE = mse, RMSE = rmse, MAD = mad))
  }, error = function(e) {
    message("Error in evaluating model: ", e$message)
    return(c(MSE = NA, RMSE = NA, MAD = NA))
  })
}

set.seed(12427550)

train_indices_50 <- tryCatch({
  sample(1:nrow(Auto), size = nrow(Auto) / 2)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in sampling for 50% training data: ", e$message)
  return(NULL)
})

if (!is.null(train_indices_50)) {
  train_data_50 <- Auto[train_indices_50, ]
  test_data_50 <- Auto[-train_indices_50, ]
  
  linear_model_50 <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ horsepower, data = train_data_50), error = function(e) NULL)
  quadratic_model_50 <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ poly(horsepower, 2), data = train_data_50), error = function(e) NULL)
  cubic_model_50 <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ poly(horsepower, 3), data = train_data_50), error = function(e) NULL)
  
  if (!is.null(linear_model_50) & !is.null(quadratic_model_50) & !is.null(cubic_model_50)) {
    results_50 <- data.frame(
      Linear = evaluate_model(linear_model_50, test_data_50),
      Quadratic = evaluate_model(quadratic_model_50, test_data_50),
      Cubic = evaluate_model(cubic_model_50, test_data_50)
    )
    print(results_50)
  } else {
    message("Error in fitting models for 50% training data")
  }
} else {
  message("Error in generating training indices for 50% training data")
}

train_indices_70 <- tryCatch({
  sample(1:nrow(Auto), size = 0.7 * nrow(Auto))
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in sampling for 70% training data: ", e$message)
  return(NULL)
})

if (!is.null(train_indices_70)) {
  train_data_70 <- Auto[train_indices_70, ]
  test_data_70 <- Auto[-train_indices_70, ]
  
  linear_model_70 <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ horsepower, data = train_data_70), error = function(e) NULL)
  quadratic_model_70 <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ poly(horsepower, 2), data = train_data_70), error = function(e) NULL)
  cubic_model_70 <- tryCatch(glm(mpg ~ poly(horsepower, 3), data = train_data_70), error = function(e) NULL)
  
  if (!is.null(linear_model_70) & !is.null(quadratic_model_70) & !is.null(cubic_model_70)) {
    results_70 <- data.frame(
      Linear = evaluate_model(linear_model_70, test_data_70),
      Quadratic = evaluate_model(quadratic_model_70, test_data_70),
      Cubic = evaluate_model(cubic_model_70, test_data_70)
    )
    print(results_70)
  } else {
    message("Error in fitting models for 70% training data")
  }
} else {
  message("Error in generating training indices for 70% training data")
}
```

The results of the validation set approach, with 50%/50% and 70%/30% train/test splits, suggest that the cubic model performs best in terms of minimizing both the Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) across both splits. Specifically, with the 50%/50% split, the cubic model achieves the lowest MSE (17.87) and RMSE (4.23), followed closely by the quadratic model. The linear model performs the worst, with an MSE of 23.01 and an RMSE of 4.80. When considering the 70%/30% split, the linear model has a slightly higher MSE (19.20) compared to the quadratic (20.05) and cubic (21.61) models, while the cubic model again shows the highest RMSE (4.65).

In terms of Median Absolute Deviation (MAD), the quadratic model performs the best in both splits, with a MAD of 2.65 for the 50%/50% split and 2.48 for the 70%/30% split. The linear model has the highest MAD value in both cases, indicating that its predictions are the least consistent.

Overall, the cubic model provides the best fit in terms of MSE and RMSE, though the quadratic model offers a better balance when considering MAD, suggesting that it may be the most robust model. The linear model, while simple, performs the worst in most metrics, particularly in the 50%/50% split. Therefore, the choice of the best model depends on the priority—whether minimizing MSE/RMSE or ensuring robustness with a lower MAD.

