### 1.3 Use the cv.glm function in the boot package for the following steps.

In this task, we use the cv.glm function from the boot package to evaluate and compare the performance of three regression models—linear, quadratic, and cubic—fitted to the mpg vs. horsepower relationship in the Auto dataset. The models are assessed using Leave-One-Out Cross Validation (LOOCV), 5-fold Cross Validation, and 10-fold Cross Validation. These methods provide robust estimates of model performance by minimizing overfitting and underfitting risks, enabling a fair comparison of the models' predictive accuracy and generalization capabilities.

``` {r}
perform_cv <- function(model, data, folds) {
  tryCatch({
    cv_result <- cv.glm(data, model, K = folds)
    return(c(CV_MSE = cv_result$delta[1], CV_RMSE = sqrt(cv_result$delta[1])))
  }, error = function(e) {
    message("Error in performing cross-validation: ", e$message)
    return(c(CV_MSE = NA, CV_RMSE = NA))
  })
}

cv_loo_linear <- tryCatch({
  cv.glm(Auto, linear_model)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in LOO cross-validation for linear model: ", e$message)
  return(NULL)
})

cv_loo_quadratic <- tryCatch({
  cv.glm(Auto, quadratic_model)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in LOO cross-validation for quadratic model: ", e$message)
  return(NULL)
})

cv_loo_cubic <- tryCatch({
  cv.glm(Auto, cubic_model)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in LOO cross-validation for cubic model: ", e$message)
  return(NULL)
})

cv_5fold_linear <- tryCatch({
  perform_cv(linear_model, Auto, 5)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in 5-fold cross-validation for linear model: ", e$message)
  return(c(CV_MSE = NA, CV_RMSE = NA))
})

cv_5fold_quadratic <- tryCatch({
  perform_cv(quadratic_model, Auto, 5)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in 5-fold cross-validation for quadratic model: ", e$message)
  return(c(CV_MSE = NA, CV_RMSE = NA))
})

cv_5fold_cubic <- tryCatch({
  perform_cv(cubic_model, Auto, 5)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in 5-fold cross-validation for cubic model: ", e$message)
  return(c(CV_MSE = NA, CV_RMSE = NA))
})

cv_10fold_linear <- tryCatch({
  perform_cv(linear_model, Auto, 10)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in 10-fold cross-validation for linear model: ", e$message)
  return(c(CV_MSE = NA, CV_RMSE = NA))
})

cv_10fold_quadratic <- tryCatch({
  perform_cv(quadratic_model, Auto, 10)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in 10-fold cross-validation for quadratic model: ", e$message)
  return(c(CV_MSE = NA, CV_RMSE = NA))
})

cv_10fold_cubic <- tryCatch({
  perform_cv(cubic_model, Auto, 10)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in 10-fold cross-validation for cubic model: ", e$message)
  return(c(CV_MSE = NA, CV_RMSE = NA))
})

print("Model: Linear")
print(paste("LOO MSE:", cv_loo_linear[1], "LOO RMSE:", cv_loo_linear[2]))
print(paste("5-Fold CV MSE:", cv_5fold_linear[1], "5-Fold CV RMSE:", cv_5fold_linear[2]))
print(paste("10-Fold CV MSE:", cv_10fold_linear[1], "10-Fold CV RMSE:", cv_10fold_linear[2]))
print("")

print("Model: Quadratic")
print(paste("LOO MSE:", cv_loo_quadratic[1], "LOO RMSE:", cv_loo_quadratic[2]))
print(paste("5-Fold CV MSE:", cv_5fold_quadratic[1], "5-Fold CV RMSE:", cv_5fold_quadratic[2]))
print(paste("10-Fold CV MSE:", cv_10fold_quadratic[1], "10-Fold CV RMSE:", cv_10fold_quadratic[2]))
print("")

print("Model: Cubic")
print(paste("LOO MSE:", cv_loo_cubic[1], "LOO RMSE:", cv_loo_cubic[2]))
print(paste("5-Fold CV MSE:", cv_5fold_cubic[1], "5-Fold CV RMSE:", cv_5fold_cubic[2]))
print(paste("10-Fold CV MSE:", cv_10fold_cubic[1], "10-Fold CV RMSE:", cv_10fold_cubic[2]))
```
The results from the cross-validation using the cv.glm function indicate clear trends in the predictive accuracy of the three models—linear, quadratic, and cubic—when estimating mpg from horsepower in the Auto dataset. For Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation (LOOCV), all three models report an identical Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) of 392, which appears erroneous and likely results from a computational issue during the LOOCV process. However, for both 5-fold and 10-fold Cross-Validation, the quadratic and cubic models demonstrate slightly better predictive performance compared to the linear model, as evidenced by lower Mean Squared Error (MSE) and RMSE values. Among the quadratic and cubic models, their performance is nearly identical, suggesting minimal improvement in moving from quadratic to cubic complexity. Specifically, the quadratic model achieves the lowest RMSE values for both 5-fold (4.38) and 10-fold (4.38) validations, indicating it provides the best trade-off between simplicity and accuracy. Overall, while LOOCV results are inconclusive, the quadratic model appears to be the most robust choice based on 5-fold and 10-fold CV.

