# Task 2

This task involves analyzing the relationship between the number of unemployed persons (unemploy) and the median duration of unemployment (uempmed) from the economics dataset in the ggplot2 package. It requires fitting a linear model, an appropriate exponential or logarithmic model (depending on variable roles), and polynomial models of varying degrees (2nd, 3rd, and 10th). Using cv.glm from the boot package, we compare these models through Leave-One-Out Cross Validation (LOOCV) and 5-fold and 10-fold Cross Validation based on Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Mean Squared Error (MSE). By visualizing the models and analyzing the cross-validation results, we explore concepts like underfitting, overfitting, and the application of cross-validation in selecting the best model.

### 2.1 Fit the following models to explain the number of unemployed persons 'unemploy' by the median number of days unemployed 'uempmed' and vice versa: linear model, an appropriate exponential or logarithmic model (which one is appropriate depends on which is the dependent or independent variable), polynomial model of 2nd, 3rd and 10th degree.

In this task, we analyze the relationship between the number of unemployed persons (unemploy) and the median duration of unemployment (uempmed) using the economics dataset from the ggplot2 package. We fit a linear model to capture a straightforward relationship, an appropriate exponential or logarithmic model based on the roles of the dependent and independent variables to explore non-linear trends, and polynomial models of 2nd, 3rd, and 10th degree to capture increasingly complex relationships. This modeling approach helps evaluate how well different types of relationships explain the data.

``` {r}
data("economics")

linear_model <- glm(unemploy ~ uempmed, data = economics)

log_model <- glm(log(unemploy) ~ uempmed, data = economics)

poly2_model <- glm(unemploy ~ poly(uempmed, 2), data = economics)
poly3_model <- glm(unemploy ~ poly(uempmed, 3), data = economics)
poly10_model <- glm(unemploy ~ poly(uempmed, 10), data = economics)
```

The above code fits the models to the data.

