The cross-validation results indicate that the logarithmic model is the most effective for predicting the relationship between unemployment duration (uempmed) and the number of unemployed (unemploy). It achieves the lowest Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) across all validation methods, with an LOOCV RMSE of 0.2305, outperforming all other models. Polynomial models, particularly higher-degree ones like the 10th degree, exhibit substantially higher RMSE and MSE, suggesting overfitting and poor generalization. Lower-degree polynomials (2nd and 3rd degree) perform better but still lag behind the logarithmic model. The linear model also demonstrates weaker performance, with high MSE and RMSE values, reflecting its inability to capture the complexity of the relationship. Overall, the logarithmic model provides the best balance of simplicity and predictive accuracy.